load balancer:
    A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers to improve availability,
    scalability, and fault tolerance using algorithms:
        round-robin
        least-connections
        hashing

    Client  ----> load balancer ----> Web Server ------> load balancer ----> Application Server ------> load balancer ----> Database Server

Characteristics
    Check health of servers regularly:  /health
            When TTL of the cached entries expires.
        Server unhealthy → removed from pool
        Server healthy → added back

Types of Load Balancers:
    1️⃣ Layer-4 Load Balancer (Transport Level):
        Operates at: TCP / UDP level
            Routes based on IP + Port

    Example
        AWS Network Load Balancer

    2️⃣ Layer-7 Load Balancer (Application Level):
        Operates at: HTTP / HTTPS
    Example:
        AWS Application Load Balancer
        Ingress Controller in Kubernetes:
            Ingress Controller is the actual Layer-7 load balancer in Kubernetes that watches Ingress
            rules and routes external traffic to internal services securely and efficiently.
        Spring Cloud Gateway

    Load Balancer in Kubernetes:
        Ingress Controller acts as a load balancer


Load Balancer ask Service Registry what are the healthy instances of service. [Where is user-service?]
    As in multi pod system instances of services changes [scale up/scale down/ deployment kill
    previous pods and bring ne pods]
     Service starts -> Registers itself  <----- Load Balancer [cache Service Registry]

     Spring ----> Eureka
     Kafka -----> Zookeeper

Kubernetes
    uses DNS as a service registry.
        DNS returns: multiple pod IPs

        DNS resolve name to IP addresses.

    | Feature           | DNS        | Service Registry |
    | ----------------- | ---------- | ---------------- |
    | Name resolution   | ✅          | ✅              |
    | Health awareness  | ❌          | ✅              |
    | Instance metadata | ❌          | ✅              |
    | Dynamic updates   | Slow (TTL) | Near real-time   |
    | Client-side LB    | Limited    | Strong           |

Local Load Balancing Vs Global Load Balancing(GSLB) (Geo / Multi-Region)

Local Load Balancing:
    It happens within a single region / cluster and usually close to the application.
    Traffic is balanced among replicas of the same service.

                    Client / Service A
                        ↓
                    Service Registry or DNS
                        ↓
                    Pick one instance

    In Kubernetes Local Load Balancing DNS returns: One ClusterIP



Global Load Balancing:
    Routes traffic across regions or data centers.
    Decision happens before traffic reaches the application.
    DNS


              User
                ↓
        Global Load Balancer DNS
                ↓
        Local Load Balancer/Regional Load Balancer: It balances traffic between: Ingress controller pods, IPs exposed by Kubernetes
                ↓
        Kubernetes Ingress Controller, it uses DNS as service registry
                ↓
        Pods (Local LB)

